* sync.Mutex

We've seen how channels are great for communication among ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000001⟫.

But what if we don't need communication? What if we just want to make sure only
one ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000002⟫ can access a variable at a time to avoid conflicts?

This concept is called ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000003⟫mutual_exclusion⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000004⟫, and the conventional name for the data structure that provides it is ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000005⟫⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000006⟫⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000007⟫.

⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000008⟫'s standard library provides mutual exclusion with
[[⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000009⟫][⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000010⟫sync.Mutex⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000011⟫]] and its two methods:

- ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000012⟫Lock⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000013⟫
- ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000014⟫Unlock⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000015⟫

We can define a block of code to be executed in mutual exclusion by surrounding it
with a call to ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000016⟫Lock⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000017⟫ and ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000018⟫Unlock⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000019⟫ as shown on the ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000020⟫Inc⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000021⟫ method.

We can also use ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000022⟫defer⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000023⟫ to ensure the ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000024⟫ will be unlocked as in the ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000025⟫Value⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000026⟫ method.

